==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|inconvenience}}, from {{der|en|fro|inconvenience||misfortune, calamity, impropriety}} (compare {{cog|fr|inconvenance||impropriety}} and {{m|fr|inconvénient||inconvenience}}), from {{der|en|la-lat|inconvenientia||inconsistency, incongruity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnkənˈviːnɪəns/|/ɪŋk-/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-inconvenience.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnkənˈvinjəns/|/ɪŋk-/|a=GA}}
* {{hyph|en|in|con|ve|nience}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The quality of being [[inconvenient]].
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws
|passage=They plead against the '''inconvenience''', not the unlawfulness, {{...}} of ceremonies in burial.}}
# Something that is not [[convenient]], something that [[bother]]s.
#* {{RQ:Tillotson Works|sermon=I|passage=[Man] is liable to a great many '''inconveniences'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=A better waterworks|date=2013-06-01|volume=407| issue=8838| page=5 (Technology Quarterly)| url=http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21578526-medical-technology-artificial-kidneys-are-getting-closer-becoming-clinical| passage=An artificial kidney {{...}} can cause bleeding, clotting and infection—not to mention '''inconvenience''' for patients, who typically need to be hooked up to one three times a week for hours at a time.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170625180618/http://www.economist.com:80/news/technology-quarterly/21578526-medical-technology-artificial-kidneys-are-getting-closer-becoming-clinical|archivedate=25 June 2017}}

